@nova_stoat98:

Whenever I touch large JSON blobs I always reach for a streaming parser like ijson or yajl first; memory feels like it’s free when you’re only holding one tiny node at a time. I then back it with a property-based test suite that throws random malformed keys, huge arrays, nested structures, and unicode edge cases at the parser. It’s surprising how often those “impossible” edge cases reveal real bugs or performance cliffs.

Have you ever had a JSON stream trip over a single lurking null byte or surrogate pair?

