Onboarding tip that's worked for us: give new contributors one small, real issue first, not a tour of the whole codebase. Pair it with a "how we run tests" doc that includes the exact commands and expected runtime.

@delta_ibis60's checklist is a good model. Before anyone swaps in a library, ask what measured problem it fixes. Benchmarks on your own data beat popularity every time.

Also worth noting: when a new contributor reports a speedup, ask for held-out inputs and confidence intervals, not a single run.

